
\section{INTRODUCTION}
\label{sec:introduction}

Federated Learning (FL) keeps raw data on clients, but standard multi-round training can still leak information through repeated gradient exchange~\cite{zhu2019deep, so2023securing}. One-shot federated learning reduces this exposure by limiting communication to a single round. However, if the transferred distillation targets are visible to the server in plaintext, a privacy risk remains~\cite{jagielski2023students}.

Differential privacy and secure aggregation partially address this risk, but each has limitations. Strong differential privacy noise degrades distillation quality~\cite{shao2023selective}, while secure aggregation protects transport without preventing the server from inspecting the aggregate~\cite{kerkouche2023client}. Homomorphic encryption (HE) provides end-to-end cryptographic protection, yet direct iterative HE training of deep models is prohibitively expensive due to multiplicative depth constraints~\cite{zhang2020batchcrypt, jin2023fedml}.

This motivates a distillation-based design that is both one-shot and HE-compatible. The main technical challenge is that HE computation supports only addition and multiplication over ciphertexts, whereas standard neural network operations---ReLU, batch normalisation, softmax---are non-polynomial. Existing work largely addresses encrypted inference; encrypted training of deep student models remains an open problem~\cite{agamennone2025polynomial, alhossain2025training}.

% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Our Approach}
We present \textbf{HE-IFD} (\textbf{H}omomorphic \textbf{E}ncryption--\textbf{I}ntermediate \textbf{F}eature \textbf{D}istillation), a framework in which individual client teacher outputs are cryptographically protected during knowledge transfer and all subsequent server-side computation. The protocol operates in three phases:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Local training.} Each of $N$ clients trains a standard ResNet-18 teacher on their private data partition, starting from a shared random initialisation.
\item \textbf{Encrypted upload.} Each client extracts per-block intermediate feature pairs from its teacher, applies client-side collaborative normalisation, encrypts the pairs under CKKS, and uploads them once.
\item \textbf{Server-side encrypted distillation.} The server trains a full-depth HE-compatible PolyResNet-18 student block by block, using only encrypted inputs and targets. Student weights, activations, gradients, and loss values are all ciphertext throughout training.
\end{enumerate}

The server never observes any individual teacher output, student weight, or intermediate activation in plaintext. It operates as a blind compute delegate that executes the distillation protocol on encrypted data.

A central technical difficulty is training an HE-compatible student---one that uses polynomial activations and ChannelScale normalisation instead of ReLU and batch normalisation---from encrypted supervision. Standard end-to-end distillation diverges for this architecture. We solve this with \emph{progressive stacking}: block-by-block training where each block receives fresh ciphertexts, combined with magnitude-regularised normalised MSE loss and student-input training. This approach yields stable convergence and competitive accuracy across our CIFAR-10 and Fashion-MNIST experiments.

% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Contributions}
Our contributions are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Progressive stacking for HE-compatible students.} We introduce a block-by-block training procedure with magnitude-regularised normalised MSE loss that enables stable distillation of a full PolyResNet-18 student (11.17M parameters, polynomial activations, ChannelScale normalisation) from encrypted teacher features.

\item \textbf{Fully encrypted one-shot distillation protocol.} Clients upload encrypted per-block feature pairs in a single round. The server performs all distillation computation---forward pass, loss evaluation, backward pass, and weight update---on ciphertexts. No plaintext data is exposed at any point during training.

\item \textbf{Client-side collaborative normalisation.} Each client normalises its teacher outputs to zero mean and unit variance per channel before encryption. This eliminates magnitude incompatibility across heterogeneous teachers and prevents training divergence for $N \geq 4$ clients.

\item \textbf{Evaluation across federated settings.} We evaluate across client counts ($N \in \{1, 2, 4, 8, 16\}$), heterogeneity levels ($\alpha \in \{0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 100.0\}$), and report accuracy, privacy analysis, and comparison with differential privacy baselines.
\end{enumerate}

The remainder of this paper is organised as follows. Section~\ref{sec:background} surveys federated learning, knowledge distillation, privacy mechanisms, and homomorphic encryption. Section~\ref{sec:methodology} presents the HE-IFD framework. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} reports experimental results. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.


